{"table":"nodes","ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/support.zero_curvature","record":{"id":"support.zero_curvature","kind":"claim","label":"Strict increase on a zero constant interval","status":"unverified","source_id":"support_tex_regularity","locator":"regularity.tex:298–308; zero case of lem:constant-transfer","statement":"For gamma in U and its controlled diffusion, if gamma=0 on an open interval I contained in (0,1), then q''(t)=E[Phi_xxx(t,X_t)^2]>0 for t in I; thus q' is strictly increasing there. A Gaussian transition from any earlier interior time gives full spatial support. Phi_xxx cannot vanish identically, because an even quadratic cannot be both one-Lipschitz and a bounded distance from |x|.","assumption_ids":[],"evidence_ids":["support_source_bindings_20261024","support_graph_semantic_review_pending"],"citation_ids":["support_transfer_source","support_gap_lean_source"],"boundary":"Curated source-level mathematical statement, not an elaborated Lean extraction or independently accepted proof. Its displayed local hypotheses are quantified guards; a hypothetical plateau is not an added premise of the full-support theorem. No division by c and no appeal to the positive-plateau certificate. For an initial interval (0,b), apply the assertion locally at each t>0 and use continuity at 0 for the final integration."},"usage":{"record_ref":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/support.zero_curvature","paper_focus":[],"paper_memberships":[{"stage":"long","subgraph_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/support/0.3.1/2f85413164f06365ea0d825befee447650da71d54080d5dd65e78a708891c560","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"short","subgraph_ref":"sha256:cfbe950801fd334d8fd957243fa3b1f716ef609148c5f2ff97d44f9a6443d82c/papers/short.json/support/0.3.1/77f0cf29ab577b42d8caa5b2831ebfb1ca170d4b13c1ef6897cadc8e93c2a834","roles":["mapped_claim"]},{"stage":"expository","subgraph_ref":"sha256:479155de953858e60f1454491ccda2215d88a4c2780e686cdfb8422481c62d9c/papers/expository.json/support/0.3.1/9dd5536bc567fc55efaa864733bf89747cea0268a8a3865dec51ddd8e2a235dd","roles":["mapped_claim"]}],"direct_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/fixed-time-coercivity","anchor":"fixed-time-coercivity","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"},{"stage":"short","passage_ref":"sha256:267b41919f95376c728c2f90f5c80e4bf7aa13ca3418d4931ff608c908902e54/papers/short.md/sha256:cfbe950801fd334d8fd957243fa3b1f716ef609148c5f2ff97d44f9a6443d82c/papers/short.json/passages/gap-proof","anchor":"gap-proof","mapping_ref":"sha256:cfbe950801fd334d8fd957243fa3b1f716ef609148c5f2ff97d44f9a6443d82c/papers/short.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"},{"stage":"expository","passage_ref":"sha256:93145ef5687bb57439b6b2cb17b3f040069f7144f8a92e8bed7d3d18237af961/papers/expository.md/sha256:479155de953858e60f1454491ccda2215d88a4c2780e686cdfb8422481c62d9c/papers/expository.json/passages/endpoints","anchor":"endpoints","mapping_ref":"sha256:479155de953858e60f1454491ccda2215d88a4c2780e686cdfb8422481c62d9c/papers/expository.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids"}]},"aligned_passages":[{"stage":"long","passage_ref":"sha256:c4249feffa26e48c24c5690f73e0de4886e40504c8c5c0e6dcb22ec94ea334da/papers/long.md/sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json/passages/fixed-time-coercivity","anchor":"fixed-time-coercivity","mapping_ref":"sha256:135459bb2961c95bd09cfce135666cd5a51fd11bb2c943829fdd7b81efdfef5d/papers/long.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n<a id=\"plateau-limit\"></a>\n\n### 4.5 A fixed-time inequality and its plateau application\n\n<a id=\"fixed-time-coercivity\"></a>\n\n**Proposition 4.1 (coercivity at a fixed time).** Let $\\gamma\\in\\mathcal U$\nand let $X$ be its associated diffusion, started at zero. Suppose that\n$0<t<1$, $\\gamma$ is continuous at $t$, and $c=\\gamma(t)>0$. With\n$a=\\Phi_{xx}$, $b=\\Phi_{xxx}$ and $d=\\Phi_{xxxx}$, evaluated at\n$(t,X_t)$, one has\n$$\n\\mathcal C_\\gamma(t):=\\mathbb E[d^2-12cab^2+6c^2a^4]\n \\ge \\frac{c^2}{1000}\\mathbb E a^4.\n\\tag{4.1}\n$$\nNeither minimality nor a constant neighbourhood of $t$ is assumed.\nThis is the common estimate behind plateau curvature and positive density.\nWhen $\\gamma$ is smooth near $t$, Itô's formula gives\n$q''=\\mathbb E b^2-2\\gamma\\mathbb E a^3$ and\n$$\n(\\mathbb E b^2)'=\\mathbb E d^2-6\\gamma\\mathbb E(ab^2),\\qquad\n(\\mathbb E a^3)'=3\\mathbb E(ab^2)-3\\gamma\\mathbb E a^4.\n$$\nDifferentiating the first identity therefore gives\n$$\nq'''(t)+2\\gamma'(t)\\mathbb E a^3=\\mathcal C_\\gamma(t).\n\\tag{4.2}\n$$\nOn a plateau the coefficient derivative vanishes. At a smooth minimizer,\n$q=t$ makes the third derivative of $q$ vanish instead.\n\n**Proof.** Fix $t<T<T'<1$ and a shrinking interval\n$[t-\\delta_n,t+\\delta_n)\\subset(0,T)$. Approximate $\\gamma$ by\n$$\nh_n=\\max\\{\\epsilon_n,\\min\\{K_n,\n                  \\eta_n\\lfloor\\gamma/\\eta_n\\rfloor\\}\\},\n$$\nwhere $0<\\epsilon_n<c<K_n$, $\\epsilon_n,\\eta_n\\downarrow0$ and\n$K_n\\uparrow\\infty$. Use $\\min(c,h_n)$ to the left of the interval,\n$c$ on it, and $\\max(c,h_n)$ to its right. Monotonicity makes these\npositive, nondecreasing, right-continuous finite-step coefficients\n$\\gamma_n$. Clipping toward $c$ outside the interval cannot increase\nthe error, and\n$$\n\\|\\gamma_n-\\gamma\\|_1\n\\le\\epsilon_n+\\eta_n+\\int_0^1(\\gamma-K_n)_+\\,ds\n +2\\delta_n\\sup_{|s-t|\\le\\delta_n}|\\gamma(s)-c|\n \\longrightarrow0.\n\\tag{4.3}\n$$\nIntegrability controls the capped tail and continuity at $t$ the last\nterm. Choose smooth terminal parameters $M_n\\ge\\max(n,K_n)$.\nThe terminal values then converge uniformly to $|x|$, and the coefficient\nmasses have a common bound. The finite-step inequality of §4.4 applies\nat $t$, where every $\\gamma_n$ equals the same positive $c$.\n\nOn a fixed strip containing $t$ and ending before one, compact-strip\nregularity gives uniform spatial convergence and bounds through order\nfour. The next derivative bounds give a common spatial Lipschitz\nconstant for those derivatives. Coupling with the same Brownian motion\nand applying Gronwall gives a deterministic bound\n$\\sup_{s\\le T}|X_s^n-X_s|\\to0$. If $D_k^n=\\partial_x^k\\Phi_n$\nand $D_k=\\partial_x^k\\Phi$, then, for $1\\le k\\le4$,\n\n$$\n|D_k^n(t,X_t^n)-D_k(t,X_t)|\n\\le\\|D_k^n-D_k\\|_{\\infty,[0,T]\\times\\mathbb R}\n   +C_{k,T}|X_t^n-X_t|\\longrightarrow0.\n$$\n\nAfter the nonnegative terms are discarded, the two surviving integrands\n$z_n^2-12v_nw_n^2+6v_n^4$ and $v_n^4$ are uniformly bounded,\nwith $v_n=cD_2^n$, $w_n=cD_3^n$ and $z_n=cD_4^n$. Dominated convergence therefore passes their expectations\nto the limit. For completeness, let $V,W,Z$ bound both the limiting and approximating\n$v,w,z$, and let $e_v,e_w,e_z$ bound their coupled evaluation errors.\nUniform spatial convergence and the path bound above give these errors\nwith $e_v,e_w,e_z\\to0$. Difference-of-powers identities give\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\mathbb E\\mathcal C(v_n,w_n,z_n)-\\mathbb E\\mathcal C(v,w,z)|\n &\\le(12W^2+24V^3)e_v+24VW e_w+2Ze_z,\\\\\n|\\mathbb E v_n^4-\\mathbb E v^4|&\\le4V^3e_v,\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhere $\\mathcal C(v,w,z)=z^2-12vw^2+6v^4$. These constants depend\non the common strip and coefficient mass bound, not on plateau width.\nAfter division by $c^2$, this is (4.1). Only spatial moments have been\npassed to the limit. This transfer requires neither convergence of $s_n$ or\n$j_n$ nor a uniform bound on their tail constants: those auxiliary\nquantities have already disappeared after integration.\n[OpenAI (2026a), Lemmas 2.1–2.3][fs-reg];\n[Proposition 5.1][fs-curv].\n\n<a id=\"fixed-time-jump-extension\"></a>\n\n**The spatial inequality at a coefficient jump.** Continuity at $t$ is\na sufficient hypothesis in Proposition 4.1 and in the retained sampled\nformal interface. The ordinary spatial-moment argument permits a little\nmore. Fix $c=\\gamma(t)>0$ and replace $\\gamma$ by $c$ on\n$[t-\\delta,t+\\delta)$, leaving it unchanged elsewhere. Monotonicity\npreserves both joins and right continuity. For fixed\n$0<\\delta_0<\\min(t,1-t)$ and $0<\\delta\\le\\delta_0$,\n$$\n\\|\\gamma_\\delta-\\gamma\\|_1\n\\le2\\delta\\,\\gamma(t+\\delta_0)\\longrightarrow0.\n$$\nThus local boundedness suffices for the same strip stability and coupled\nexpectation limit. Each replacement has a positive plateau at $t$, so\n(4.1) holds at a jump too, with the stated right-continuous value $c$.\nThis extension concerns spatial moments only: it supplies no time\nderivative $q'''(t)$ or $\\gamma'(t)$ at a jump and does not remove a\nhypothesis from the retained Lean declaration. Neither application below\nrequires this extension.\n\n**Corollary 4.2 (positive plateaus).** Suppose now that $\\gamma=c>0$\non an open interval $I\\subset(0,1)$ and $t\\in I$. Then (4.2) gives\n$q'''(t)=\\mathcal C_\\gamma(t)$. To establish strictness for this\nlimiting solution, choose $b\\in I$ later than $t$ and put\n$\\psi=\\Phi_\\gamma(b,\\cdot)$. This is smooth, convex, even and a\nbounded distance from $|x|$, so $\\psi'$ cannot be constant. The\nCole–Hopf formula gives\n\n$$\n\\Phi_{xx}(t,x)\n=\\mathbb E^{\\rm tilt}_{t,x}\\psi''\n  +c\\operatorname{Var}^{\\rm tilt}_{t,x}(\\psi'),\n$$\n$$\n\\nu_{t,x}(dy)=\n\\frac{e^{c\\psi(y)}p_{b-t}(y-x)}\n {\\int_{\\mathbb R}e^{c\\psi(z)}p_{b-t}(z-x)\\,dz}\\,dy.\n$$\n\nThe tilted density is positive everywhere. Its variance of the\nnonconstant continuous function $\\psi'$ is strictly positive; its\nmean of $\\psi''$ is nonnegative. Thus $v(t,x)>0$ for every $x$, and\n$\\mathbb E v(t,X_t)^4>0$. Combining the limiting inequality with\nthe differentiated identity proves\n$q'''(t)\\ge(1000c^2)^{-1}\\mathbb E v(t,X_t)^4>0$.\nThis proves strict convexity of $q'$ on $I$.\n[OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 2.3][fs-reg]; [Proposition 5.1][fs-curv].\n\n<a id=\"zero-plateau\"></a>\n\n### 4.6 Why the zero interval remains a separate case\n\nWhen $c=0$, the heat equation gives\n$q''(t)=\\mathbb E\\Phi_{xxx}(t,X_t)^2$ without division by $c$.\nStart the drift-free evolution at an earlier point of $I$; its\nGaussian transition kernel makes the law of $X_t$ have a positive\ndensity everywhere. If the expectation were zero, continuity would\nforce $\\Phi_{xxx}(t,\\cdot)\\equiv0$. Then $\\Phi(t,\\cdot)$ would\nbe an even quadratic polynomial. Its one-Lipschitz bound forces that\npolynomial to be constant, contradicting its bounded distance from\n$|x|$. Therefore $q''(t)>0$, giving strict increase of $q'$.\n[OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 2.3][fs-reg].\n\nThe analytic certificate is thus a proved input to the gap argument in\nthis source, with the stated model and regularity assumptions. An\nabstract polynomial or flux lemma assumes those analytic hypotheses;\nthe finite-step comparisons and the transfer argument discharge them.\nInspection of the corresponding Lean sources records that construction,\nwhile compilation, scoped dependency extraction and Comparator replay\nremain distinct checks, with their observed outcomes reported in §13.\n","span":{"start_byte":62293,"end_byte":69498,"start_line":1363,"end_line":1528},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/long?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=fixed-time-coercivity","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=long&passage=fixed-time-coercivity"},{"stage":"short","passage_ref":"sha256:267b41919f95376c728c2f90f5c80e4bf7aa13ca3418d4931ff608c908902e54/papers/short.md/sha256:cfbe950801fd334d8fd957243fa3b1f716ef609148c5f2ff97d44f9a6443d82c/papers/short.json/passages/gap-proof","anchor":"gap-proof","mapping_ref":"sha256:cfbe950801fd334d8fd957243fa3b1f716ef609148c5f2ff97d44f9a6443d82c/papers/short.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n**Lemma 2 (constant-interval curvature; [OpenAI (2026a),\nProposition 5.1][FS]).** Let $\\gamma\\in\\mathcal U$, with its\nassociated diffusion and moment $q$. If $\\gamma=c$ on an open\ninterval $I\\subset(0,1)$, called a plateau, then for $t\\in I$\n$$\nq'''(t)\\geq\\frac{c^2}{1000}\\mathbb E[a(t,X_t)^4]>0\n\\quad\\text{if }c>0.                               \\tag{3}\n$$\nIf $c=0$, then $q''(t)=\\mathbb E[\\Phi_{xxx}(t,X_t)^2]>0$.\n\nMinimality is not a hypothesis of this lemma. Section 3 derives its\nmoment polynomial and explains the integrated estimate imported from\nthe source.\n\n**Proof of Theorem 1, assuming Lemma 2.** A complementary\ninterval with two support endpoints admits two contact conditions;\nan initial interval without support at zero has coefficient zero.\nFor terminal gaps, the bound $q\\leq1$ replaces the missing right\ncontact condition.\n\nSuppose $(\\ell,r)$ is a support gap with endpoints in $S$ and $r<1$.\nThe coefficient is a positive constant there: support at $\\ell$ and\nabsence of mass immediately to its right force\n$\\mu_\\gamma([0,\\ell])>0$. Strict convexity of $q'$ and (2) give\n$$\nq'(t)<\\frac{r-t}{r-\\ell}q'(\\ell)\n       +\\frac{t-\\ell}{r-\\ell}q'(r)\\leq1\n\\qquad(\\ell<t<r).\n$$\nIntegrating contradicts $q(r)-q(\\ell)=r-\\ell$. The geometric\nobstruction is an area deficit under $q'$.\nAn initial gap $[0,r)$ with $0\\notin S$ and $r\\in S$ has no\nmass, hence $\\gamma=0$ there. Its right endpoint gives $q(r)=r$\nand $q'(r)\\leq1$, while zero field gives $q(0)=0$.\nThe zero-plateau identity gives $q''>0$, hence $q'(t)<q'(r)\\leq1$.\nThe same\nintegral contradiction follows without assuming $G(0)=0$. A gap\n$(0,r)$ with $0\\in S$ is already covered by the two-contact case.\n\nA terminal gap $(r,1)$ with nonempty support has $r\\in S$ and\npositive plateau height. Suppose $q(t)\\geq t$ for some $r<t<1$.\nStrict convexity of $q'$ and $q'(r)\\leq1$ give\n$$\nt-r\\leq\\int_r^t q'(s)\\,ds\n<\\frac{t-r}{2}\\bigl(q'(r)+q'(t)\\bigr),\n$$\nso $q'(t)>1$. Convexity then gives $q'(s)>q'(t)>1$ for $s>t$.\nConsequently $q(s)>t+(s-t)q'(t)>1$ for $s<1$ sufficiently\nclose to one, contrary to $q\\leq1$. Thus $q(t)<t$ on $(r,1)$,\nand $G(r)<0$ contradicts minimality. This uses no contact at one.\n\nIf $S$ is empty, then $\\gamma=0$. Lemma 2 makes $q$ strictly\nconvex, and $q(0)=0$, $q\\leq1$ give, for $0<t<b<1$,\n$q(t)<(t/b)q(b)\\leq t/b$. Letting $b\\uparrow1$ gives\n$q(t)\\leq t$. Applying strict convexity again with $t<b<1$\nnow gives $q(t)<(t/b)q(b)\\leq t$, so $G(0)<0$.\n\nThe terminal exclusion was already recorded by\n[Auffinger, Chen and Zeng (2020), Remark 7][ACZ]. The argument\nhere uses only the curvature needed for the other gaps;\n<span class=\"pdf-companion\" data-pdf-target=\"../long/reasoning-record.pdf\" data-pdf-anchor=\"qualitative-terminal-gap\">[long §5, “A shorter qualitative exclusion”](../long/reasoning-record.md#qualitative-terminal-gap)</span>\ngives the full endpoint discussion. The stronger boundary estimate\n$1-q(t)\\geq C\\sqrt{1-t}$ for arbitrary coefficients constant\nnear one is proved in [OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 6.3][FS] and\n<span class=\"pdf-companion\" data-pdf-target=\"../long/reasoning-record.pdf\" data-pdf-anchor=\"terminal-gap\">[long §5, terminal boundary estimate](../long/reasoning-record.md#terminal-gap)</span>;\n[Chen (2026), Proposition 4.5, equations (4.27)–(4.38)][Chen]\ngives the square-root asymptotic under the terminal-gap hypothesis.\nThe zero-coefficient heat argument and late-step variation have\nantecedents in [Chen, Handschy and Lerman (2018), Theorem 6][CHL].\n\nThese cases exhaust the complement of the relatively closed set $S$.\nConsequently $S=[0,1)$, contact gives $q(t)=t$, and differentiation\ngives $\\mathbb E a^2=1$, including the right-hand value at zero.\nMoreover,\n$\\gamma(t_2)-\\gamma(t_1)=\\mu_\\gamma((t_1,t_2])>0$.\nThis proves Theorem 1 using the stated analytic inputs.\n","span":{"start_byte":12109,"end_byte":15908,"start_line":248,"end_line":324},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/short?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=short&passage=gap-proof","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=short&passage=gap-proof"},{"stage":"expository","passage_ref":"sha256:93145ef5687bb57439b6b2cb17b3f040069f7144f8a92e8bed7d3d18237af961/papers/expository.md/sha256:479155de953858e60f1454491ccda2215d88a4c2780e686cdfb8422481c62d9c/papers/expository.json/passages/endpoints","anchor":"endpoints","mapping_ref":"sha256:479155de953858e60f1454491ccda2215d88a4c2780e686cdfb8422481c62d9c/papers/expository.json","mapping_field":"claim_ids","text":"\n### 5.4 The remaining initial and terminal gaps\n\n**Initial gap with zero outside the support.** Suppose $[0,s)$ is\nmissing and $s<1$ is the first support point. Then $\\gamma=0$ there,\nso the positive-coefficient bound supplies no strictness. Instead the\nzero-coefficient heat identity gives\n\n$$\nq''(t)=\\mathbb E[\\Phi_{xxx}(t,X_t)^2]>0\\qquad(0<t<s).\n$$\n\nHere is why the inequality is strict. On this interval $X_t=B_t$,\nwhose density is positive everywhere at positive times. If the expected\nsquare were zero, continuity would force $\\Phi_{xxx}(t,x)=0$ for\nevery $x$: a nonzero value would give a small interval on which the\nsquare is positive, and that interval has positive probability.\nThen $\\Phi(t,\\cdot)$ would be a quadratic polynomial, and its bounded\nslope would make it affine, a linear function plus a constant.\nBut choosing zero control gives\n$\\Phi_\\gamma(t,x)\\ge\\mathbb E|x+B_1-B_t|\\ge|x|$: the average\nabsolute value is at least the absolute value of the average. This\nrules out an affine value: domination of $|x|$ in the positive tail\nrequires slope at least one, while the negative tail requires slope\nat most minus one. The heat identity and the needed regularity are\nin [OpenAI (2026a), Lemmas 2.2–2.3][support-regularity].\n\nThus $q'$ is strictly increasing, and right contact gives\n$q'(t)<q'(s)\\le1$ for $0<t<s$. Symmetry supplies $q(0)=0$,\nwhile contact gives $q(s)=s$. Integrating again gives too little area.\nStrictness was needed only at positive times; symmetry can make the\nthird spatial derivative vanish at $(0,0)$.\n\nAlthough the forward path is Brownian here, the backward value receives\ndata $\\Phi_\\gamma(s,\\cdot)$ from the later coefficient. The formula\nfor the globally zero candidate cannot be reused on this initial\ninterval. The case where zero itself is supported was already included\nin Section 5.3, using its atom and the one-sided slope bound.\n\n```{=latex}\n\\noindent\\begin{minipage}{\\linewidth}\n```\n\n**Empty support.** This would mean $\\gamma=0$ everywhere. The preceding\nheat argument now gives $q''>0$ throughout $(0,1)$: it is $q$ itself\nthat is strictly convex. Recall that $q(0)=0$ and $q\\le1$. For\n$0<t<s<1$, the chord from zero to $s$ gives\n\n$$\nq(t)\\le\\frac ts q(s)\\le\\frac ts.\n$$\n\nLetting $s$ increase to one gives $q(t)\\le t$; only the numerical\nbound $t/s$ is taken to a limit. To obtain strictness, choose $t<v<1$\nand compare with the chord ending at $v$:\n\n$$\nq(t)<\\frac tv q(v)\\le t.\n$$\n\nHence $G(0)=\\int_0^1(q(t)-t)\\,dt<0$, contrary to the allowed\naddition of mass at zero. No value or derivative at one was used.\nSection 7.1 also computes the exact first variation\n$D\\mathcal P(0)[1]=1/4-1/\\pi$.\n\n```{=latex}\n\\end{minipage}\n```\n\n**Terminal gap.** Now suppose a maximal missing interval is $(r,1)$.\nIts left endpoint $r<1$ is supported, and its constant height is\npositive, by the same mass argument as in Section 5.3. We have\n$q(r)=r$ and $q'(r)\\le1$, and $q'$ is strictly convex on $(r,1)$.\nThere is no contact condition at one. We use the bound $q\\le1$\ninstead, recalling that $q=\\mathbb E[u^2]$ and $|u|\\le1$.\nWe will show that $q$ stays below the diagonal throughout the gap,\nmaking the tail cost $G(r)$ negative. The proof has two steps.\n\n**Reaching the diagonal forces a slope above one.**\nPut $h(t)=q'(t)-1$, so $h(r)\\le0$. If $q(t)\\ge t$ at some\n$r<t<1$, strict integration below the chord on the finite interval\n$[r,t]$ would give\n\n$$\n0\\le q(t)-t=\\int_r^t h(v)\\,dv\n<\\frac{t-r}{2}\\bigl(h(r)+h(t)\\bigr)\n\\le\\frac{t-r}{2}h(t).\n$$\n\nHence $h(t)>0$.\n\n**The excess slope persists and violates the bound.**\nTo control a later slope, use the same chord rule\non the larger interval $[r,s]$, where $t<s<1$. At its interior\npoint $t$ this gives\n\n$$\nh(t)\\le\\frac{s-t}{s-r}h(r)+\\frac{t-r}{s-r}h(s).\n$$\n\nSolving for the right endpoint gives\n\n$$\nh(s)\\ge h(t)+\\frac{s-t}{t-r}\\bigl(h(t)-h(r)\\bigr)\\ge h(t)>0.\n$$\n\nThe bound holds at every time after $t$, so integration gives\n$q(s)\\ge t+(s-t)(1+h(t))$. Choose any time\n\n$$\nt+\\frac{1-t}{1+h(t)}<s<1.\n$$\n\nSuch a choice exists because $h(t)>0$, and the lower bound now gives\n$q(s)>1$, contradicting $q(s)\\le1$ strictly before time one.\nThus $q(t)<t$ throughout $(r,1)$, giving\n$G(r)=\\int_r^1(q(t)-t)\\,dt<0$, contrary to the addition test.\nOnly finite-interval convexity and the bound on $q$ were used; no\nvalue for $q'(1)$ was assumed.\n\nThe source's different terminal argument proves the stronger estimate\n$1-q(t)\\ge C\\sqrt{1-t}$ near one for any finite constant terminal\ncoefficient, without the left contact data used here. Its proof is\n[OpenAI (2026a), Lemma 6.3][support-variation] and is retained in the\n*Long Reasoning Record*,\n<span class=\"pdf-companion\" data-pdf-target=\"../long/reasoning-record.pdf\" data-pdf-anchor=\"terminal-gap\">[§5, terminal estimate](../long/reasoning-record.md#terminal-gap)</span>.\n[Auffinger–Chen–Zeng, Remark 7][acz] had already recorded the absence\nof terminal plateaus.\n\nThese cases exhaust the complement of the support. Contact now gives\n$q(t)=t$ for every interior time, and symmetry includes zero.\nDifferentiating gives $\\mathbb E[a(t,X_t)^2]=1$, completing the theorem.\n","span":{"start_byte":35829,"end_byte":40936,"start_line":844,"end_line":975},"paper_href":"/api/v1/papers/expository?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20","reading_href":"/api/v1/papers/reading?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=expository&passage=endpoints","query_href":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&paper=expository&passage=endpoints"}],"lenses":{"prerequisites":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=support.zero_curvature&assumptions=true","consumers":"/api/v1/graph?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&view=arguments&node=support.zero_curvature&direction=consumers&assumptions=true","direct_passages":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=support.zero_curvature&relation=direct","prerequisite_uses":"/api/v1/papers/query?manifest=41f03ec0a8b1d30d68310120008da72a73f8c3f4cc88d65acbde651f5ad06a20&node=support.zero_curvature&relation=prerequisites"},"relations":[{"family":"arguments","id":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/edges/%5B%22support.empty_support_chord_obstruction%22%2C%22support.zero_curvature%22%2C%22supports%22%5D","from":"sha256:9a43c3eddb32c8ae6201629ef2e4df2d94167dc286a45dd429a8dff192df88b5/nodes/support.empty_support_chord_obstruction","to":"sha256:9a43